### What is the Maximum Subarray Sum with One Deletion problem?

Given an array of integers, find the maximum sum of a contiguous non-empty subarray where you may delete at most one element from the subarray. This is LeetCode 1186.

For example, given `[1, -2, 0, 3]`, the maximum sum without deletion is 3 (just `[3]`), but with one deletion we can remove -2 from `[1, -2, 0, 3]` to get `[1, 0, 3]` with sum 4.

### How do the two states work?

At each index, we maintain two values.

- `noDel`: the maximum sum of a subarray ending here with no deletion.
- `oneDel`: the maximum sum of a subarray ending here with exactly one deletion used somewhere inside it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxSubarrayOneDeleteService {

    /**
     * Finds the best P&L run allowing one outlier day to be struck out.
     * Used by the National Bank of India risk management team.
     */
    public static long maxSumWithOneDeletion(long[] pnl) {
        long noDel = pnl[0];
        long oneDel = 0; // No deletion possible with a single element
        long globalMax = pnl[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < pnl.length; i++) {
            // Snapshot noDel before overwriting
            long prevNoDel = noDel;

            // Standard Kadane for noDel
            noDel = Math.max(pnl[i], noDel + pnl[i]);

            // oneDel: extend existing deletion, or delete current element
            oneDel = Math.max(oneDel + pnl[i], prevNoDel);

            // Track best across both states
            globalMax = Math.max(globalMax, Math.max(noDel, oneDel));
        }
        return globalMax;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] pnl = {10000, -50000, 20000, 30000, -5000};
        System.out.println("Best run (with one strike-out): Rs."
            + maxSumWithOneDeletion(pnl));
        System.out.println("Best run (no strike-out): Rs."
            + maxSubarraySum(pnl));
    }

    private static long maxSubarraySum(long[] arr) {
        long cur = arr[0], best = arr[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            cur = Math.max(arr[i], cur + arr[i]);
            best = Math.max(best, cur);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

